1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 10, continuous testing in Jenkins.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
The idea is simple.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Each test stage runs a command.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:18,000
When pytest finds a failing test, it exits with a non zero code, Jenkins marks the stage failed, and every later stage is skipped.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:30,000
These are the lines of the Jenkinsfile that do it: the two test commands, the report publishing that always runs, and the clean up line at the start.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Notice that package, images, publish and deploy come after the tests.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:47,000
To prove the quality gate, I introduced a deliberate defect on a throw away branch called experiment slash defect demo.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
It has two commits: first the defect, then the corrective fix.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
And this second command proves the safety: it lists the branches that contain the defect commit.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Only the two experiment branches.

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
The defect was never merged into develop or main.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:09,000
This is the defect.

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:18,000
In the overlap rule of the booking data access layer, the strict comparison, check in less than check out, became less than or equal.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:27,000
A tiny off by one change, but it wrongly says that a room is booked when one guest checks out on the same day another checks in.

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
A subtle bug like this is exactly what automated tests exist to catch.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Now I reproduce what Jenkins saw, safely.

17
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:48,000
I clone the repository into a disposable folder, check out the defect commit, and run the same tests the pipeline runs.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
A test fails, the summary says failed, and the exit code is one.

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
That non zero code is exactly what makes Jenkins stop.

20
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Nothing in my real project changed.

21
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Now the corrective commit.

22
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
I check out the fix in the same disposable clone and run the same tests.

23
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
They all pass, and the exit code is zero.

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
In Jenkins, this is build number three: green, and the deployment stage runs.

25
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The fix restored the strict comparison, one line in one file.

26
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:48,000
I delete the disposable clone and confirm that my project is untouched: same branch, and no new changes except this demonstration folder.

27
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Studying build two, I found a real problem.

28
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Its test report listed five Selenium tests as passed, although the Selenium stage had been skipped.

29
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Jenkins reuses its workspace, so the old report from build one was still there and was published again.

30
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
The fix, on branch fix slash jenkins stale reports, is this: the checkout stage now deletes the reports and dist folders first.

31
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
A build can no longer publish a report from an older build.

32
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
One last detail you may be asked about.

33
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:39,000
The Jenkins builds were made before the history was rewritten, so Jenkins and the evidence logs show the original commit IDs.

34
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:51,000
This map links each original ID to the current one: the defect commit, the corrective fix, and the stale report fix are all listed, with the same subject lines.

35
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Same changes, new hash.


